meta {
  name: Remove Push Subscription
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

delete {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/notifications/push-subscriptions
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{notifAdminAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "endpoint": "{{pushEndpoint}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 204 No Content", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(204);
  });
}

docs {
  # DELETE /notifications/push-subscriptions — withdraw this browser's subscription (PAC-154 PR4)

  Authenticated, **no permission**, scoped to the caller. Run **Register Push
  Subscription** first — it captures `pushEndpoint`.

  ⚠ **The endpoint travels in the request body, not the path.** A push
  endpoint is a long URL with `/` and `%` in it, and nginx's `proxy_pass` with
  a URI part re-normalises encoded slashes — `DELETE …/push-subscriptions/:endpoint`
  would arrive mangled behind the production proxy. The ticket's table wrote
  the path form; this is the deliberate deviation.

  A **soft delete**: the row gets `deletedAt` and stays (PAC-155 — nothing is
  hard-deleted). The worker does the same on a `404`/`410` from the push
  service. Somebody else's endpoint — and one already withdrawn — is a `404`,
  not a `403`, so an endpoint cannot be probed for whose it is.

  ## Body
  ```json
  { "endpoint": "https://fcm.googleapis.com/fcm/send/…" }
  ```

  ## Response `204`
  Empty.

  ## Errors
  - `400` — body without a valid `https` endpoint.
  - `401` — no session.
  - `404` — not the caller's live subscription.
}
